  "stated_proposed_monthly_cost_usd": 447.78,
  "justification": "The original architecture cannot sustain the N1 peak-load requirement of 750 req/sec at the origin. With t3.micro instances (40 req/sec each at 70% CPU) and only 2 instances minimum, the baseline is 80 req/sec—far below 750. Scaling to 19+ instances would exceed any reasonable maximum and create cost explosion. The db.t3.micro database becomes a bottleneck under peak load and the stated bill of materials shows single-AZ pricing, failing N2 HA and N3 recovery objectives.\n\nProposed design: Upgrade EC2 to t3.small (80 req/sec each). With ASG minimum 2 and maximum 10, the fleet scales from 160 req/sec baseline to 800 req/sec peak capacity, exceeding the 750 req/sec requirement while remaining cost-effective. Upgrade database to db.t3.medium Multi-AZ with 7-day backup retention and point-in-time recovery enabled, meeting N2 automatic failover, N3 RPO ≤ 5 minutes (continuous backup), and RTO ≤ 1 hour (Multi-AZ failover is automatic and fast).\n\nCost calculation: EC2 (1460 h × $0.0208 = $30.37), ALB hour (730 × $0.0225 = $16.43) + LCU (1460 × $0.008 = $11.68), NAT gateway hour (730 × $0.045 = $32.85) + data (100 × $0.045 = $4.50), RDS db.t3.medium Multi-AZ (1460 × $0.068 × 2 = $198.40) + storage Multi-AZ (20 × $0.115 × 2 = $4.60), S3 (50 × $0.023 = $1.15), CloudFront (2000 × $0.085 = $170.00 + 4000 × $0.01 = $40.00) = $447.78. This is well within the $750 budget (N5), leaving $302.22 headroom.\n\nWell-Architected Framework: Operational Excellence (automated Multi-AZ failover, 7-day retention for point-in-time recovery); Security (private subnets, public ALB only); Reliability (N2–N3 HA/DR explicit, 2 AZs, Auto Scaling, Multi-AZ database with automatic failover); Performance Efficiency (right-sized t3.small instances deliver 80 req/sec each, CloudFront cache hit ratio reduces origin load by 50%); Cost Optimization (within budget, no over-provisioning, no unnecessary read replicas).",
  "comparison": "Original architecture (Monthly: $309.90): t3.micro instances (2 minimum, 4 maximum = 80–160 req/sec capacity) cannot reach 750 req/sec origin load required by N1; ASG would need to scale to 19+ instances, creating cost explosion beyond budget. Database is db.t3.micro with single-AZ pricing in the stated bill-of-materials, failing N2 and N3 requirements. The t3.micro database class is severely underpowered for peak load.\n\nProposed architecture (Monthly: $447.78): t3.small instances (2 minimum, 10 maximum = 160–800 req/sec capacity) exceed 750 req/sec peak requirement with headroom. Database is db.t3.medium Multi-AZ with PITR and 7-day retention, explicitly meeting N2 automatic failover and N3 RPO ≤ 5 min, RTO ≤ 1 hour. Cost is $137.88 higher than the original but well below the $750 budget cap (N5 compliance). Scalability is achieved without runaway cost; reliability is guaranteed by architecture."
}
