  "justification": "The original architecture on Stage 3 is severely undersized for the new workload. Peak demand of 750 RPS (after CloudFront cache hit) cannot be sustained by 2 × t3.micro instances (80 RPS capacity at 70% CPU each = 160 RPS total), creating a critical scalability gap. The database bottleneck is equally severe: db.t3.micro offers minimal throughput and the current Single-AZ configuration with Multi-AZ replication enabled incurs the 2× multiplier cost without proper capacity. The proposed architecture scales the web tier to t3.small instances (80 RPS each per instance), allowing the Auto Scaling group to grow from minimum 2 to maximum 10 instances, providing up to 800 RPS capacity—sufficient to handle 750 RPS peak with headroom for burst and failover scenarios. The database is upgraded to db.t3.medium (providing ~4× the throughput of micro), with Multi-AZ enabled for automatic failover, point-in-time recovery enabled (7-day retention satisfies the 5-minute RPO and 1-hour RTO requirements via automated backups), and storage doubled to 40 GB to accommodate growth. NAT gateway, ALB, and CloudFront remain unchanged as they are already correctly sized. CloudFront caching efficiency (50% cache hit ratio) is leveraged to reduce origin load. The total monthly cost of $698.47 stays within the $750 budget with a $51.53 margin, accommodating operational overhead and minor scaling adjustments. This design provides the minimum viable capacity to meet N1–N6.",
